WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.700
Good morning, John.
So, as you can tell by the headphones,

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.180
the different camera angle, the length
of the video, we're doing something

00:00:07.189 --> 00:00:11.870
a little bit different today. I wanted to
take an opportunity to talk to somebody who

00:00:11.870 --> 00:00:17.870
is a part of this community, watched her first
Vlogbrothers video in 2007, and also is just

00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:23.360
a really amazing person who does a lot of amazing
work doing advocacy, and activism, and strategy,

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:28.980
and writing. Most recently, she was the campaign
manager for Julian Castro. She's now working

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:32.250
with Elizabeth Warren. I think we're really
lucky to have her as part of this community.

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:36.140
So I wanted to take a little bit of time to
talk to Maya Rupert. Obviously it's a hard time,

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:41.100
so Maya, I guess I'll just start out
by asking, how are you feeling right now?

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:44.840
[Maya:]
This is a hard time. And this has been a very hard time,

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:50.440
even as I feel like I have been someone,
I've worked in these spaces a lot,

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:57.489
and I feel like I have a pretty high capacity
for, "Things are going badly, but there's a

00:00:57.489 --> 00:01:01.400
light at the end of the tunnel" and that kind
of thing. These last couple of weeks have

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:08.100
hit me really hard. And I know they're hitting
a lot of people really hard. And so, I'm hanging in,

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:11.760
I'm taking a lot of solace, in the fact
that I know a lot of people are feeling this

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:15.960
right now and are making community where they
can, and are being there for each other, which

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:22.080
is always the thing that I can take solace
in, in these moments, because that gives me

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:25.860
hope that there is somewhere we're trying
to go with this. But I mean, this is a tough

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:28.180
time and this one really does feel different.

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:32.900
[Hank:]
From the perspective inside of

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:40.580
presidential politics at this point,
which is a world of its own, I'm sure,

00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:45.620
can you give me a little bit of a feeling for what
it looks like from that perspective?

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:49.240
[Maya:]
A lot of leaders right now are looking to,

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:55.800
"What can we put energy behind, and what can
people be doing?" And I think that is helpful.

00:01:55.810 --> 00:02:00.870
I think that people want to feel right now,
there's something to do and ways that they

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:07.090
can act. One of the things I'm always struck
by when we talk about police violence, is

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:11.670
that people feel... There's the hopelessness
that comes with it that I think is understandable,

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:16.689
but then there's a piece of hopelessness that
I've never quite been able to square because

00:02:16.689 --> 00:02:20.540
there's the sense of, "Well, what can we do?"
There's only so much that can be done.

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:26.659
But the thing is that a system that works like
this, this was policy that makes it possible

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:33.880
for police officers to act in dangerous ways,
and not be held accountable.

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:36.260
So there are policy solutions to it.

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:40.659
So I think,
sometimes there's a disconnect.

00:02:40.659 --> 00:02:44.599
I feel like people aren't as conscious of
that as they should be. And so I'm really

00:02:44.599 --> 00:02:49.019
heartened by all the work that people are
doing right now to point to policy suggestions that

00:02:49.019 --> 00:02:55.140
are about what can happen, national use of
force standards requiring cops to deescalate

00:02:55.140 --> 00:03:00.230
before any kind of fatal force is used. Some
of these things that will have huge, huge impacts

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:07.469
on these situations, moving forward.
So I think that we do have a moment from just

00:03:07.469 --> 00:03:12.049
a political standpoint to, this is when we
need to be calling electives, letting people

00:03:12.049 --> 00:03:17.180
know that this is a priority and making sure
that at every level of government, local,

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:20.459
state, federal, this is something that people
are are prioritizing and we're getting

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:24.640
folks in office that are going to take this
issue seriously.

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:30.160
[Hank:]
You and I are about the same age, so we're...

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:35.320
we've lived through some history at this point.
[Maya:] Right.

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:39.940
[Hank:] But I'm wondering how you feel
about the youths right now,

00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:44.120
and if that is a source of any
encouragement for you right now.

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:49.950
[Maya:]
I'm always taken by young people, and how

00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:58.919
people are activating in these moments of
crisis. I'm so excited to see how people are

00:03:58.919 --> 00:04:05.959
looking at this and demanding more. And I
think it's always a tough thing generationally,

00:04:05.959 --> 00:04:12.099
when we're doing move at work or in organizing,
I think that there's this, just by nature

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:16.700
of how these things work, as you get older,
there are more things that you have lived through,

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:22.500
you've seen things work so differently.
And so you're willing to put up with things

00:04:22.509 --> 00:04:25.639
that younger people aren't. And I think,
one thing that I always try to

00:04:25.639 --> 00:04:31.530
encourage people it's, as you become the older
people in the movement, try to have the humility

00:04:31.530 --> 00:04:35.210
to recognize that the movement is changing.
And as you are the younger people in the movement,

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:40.520
have the grace to realize that the people
who have gone through it, they just had different

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:44.560
formative experiences. And so I think I'm
definitely at that point of the movement now,

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:52.870
where I'm asking for the grace and calling
on my humility. But what is, I think, really

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:58.220
cool about that cycle is that it means you
get to watch this new generation of people

00:04:58.220 --> 00:05:02.640
who look at this and say,

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:05.490
"This far, no further,
and we are not compromising."

00:05:05.490 --> 00:05:09.090
And so I'm really excited
that I'm getting

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:15.240
to see this generation take this issue on,
because it is one that needs that kind of

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:20.580
attention, that kind of demand, that kind
of unwillingness to compromise.

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:26.199
I'm seeing that a lot, I'm seeing that with the folks
that have taken to the streets. And it's weird,

00:05:26.199 --> 00:05:31.289
because there's an urgency to it, and I really
have a lot of faith in this generation of

00:05:31.289 --> 00:05:35.800
organizers to really make sure that as much
as this moment feels different,

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:37.860
that this actually can be
different this time.

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:40.699
[Hank:]
I think that it's important that we have fuels

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:47.300
that burn hot, and we have fuels that burn
long. I try to keep that as an anchor in my head,

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:54.500
that both of those things are vital.
Are there things that look practical,

00:05:54.500 --> 00:06:00.800
you were talking about this a little bit earlier,
practical things that we are imagining paths

00:06:00.810 --> 00:06:07.590
forward, actual policies that we need to be
working on. What does that look like?

00:06:07.590 --> 00:06:10.479
[Maya:]
There is a campaign that literally just launched

00:06:10.479 --> 00:06:14.460
two days ago. And whenever this comes up,
maybe it's longer than that.

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:18.960
[Hank:] Three days ago.
[Maya:] Yes, yes! It literally just launched.

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:23.940
Campaign Zero launched this initiative that
they're calling 8 Can't Wait.

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:28.580
And I'm obsessed
with it because it is, I feel like exactly

00:06:28.590 --> 00:06:33.370
what was needed at this moment. People want
to see solutions, they want to be able to

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:38.750
advocate. And we also recognize, unfortunately
that we just don't have the leadership at

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:44.190
the federal level for this to get taken seriously.
And so, understanding all of that, what they

00:06:44.190 --> 00:06:51.580
did is put together a list of eight demands
that we can call our local leaders,

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:58.280
call your mayors, these are things that can happen at
the local level. They do not require congressional action.

00:06:58.280 --> 00:07:02.199
These are actions, because
I think the interesting thing, and one of

00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:06.680
the tough things about regulating policing
in this country is that it is patchwork,

00:07:06.680 --> 00:07:12.160
it's so based on cities. The other side though, of that,
is that we can engage in this kind of advocacy

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:17.060
and people can call mayors, and
you can go to their website and actually see,

00:07:17.060 --> 00:07:21.240
based on your city, which of these policies
your city already has in place,

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:23.440
which ones still need to happen,
who you can call.

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:26.640
What they've put together
I think is, for a lot of people,

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:30.020
exactly what we were hoping for,
exactly what we were looking for.

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:34.500
It's something tangible that people can be working
toward, and it's something that really will

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:39.430
make a difference. I've seen in the last couple
of days, Vice President Biden, talking about

00:07:39.430 --> 00:07:44.200
this stuff a lot more, like these are the things
that will make a difference. Banning chokeholds,

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:50.320
requiring just a universal national use of
force so that it's not whatever police officer

00:07:50.330 --> 00:07:54.669
wants to do in a particular moment. There's
got to be more accountability for how people

00:07:54.669 --> 00:07:56.919
respond in a moment.
And that's what this stuff is about.

00:07:56.919 --> 00:07:58.689
I mean, honestly,
more than anything,

00:07:58.689 --> 00:08:05.340
this is the kind of issue that will change because
there's political will. And so, finally talking

00:08:05.340 --> 00:08:10.650
about this like it is a critical central issue.
I think so many times when we talk about things

00:08:10.650 --> 00:08:15.900
like police violence or anything that touches
on racial justice, we treat it like...

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:19.400
it sort of gets cordoned off

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:24.410
and treated like it's an issue for one community.
And when something like this happens,

00:08:24.410 --> 00:08:28.449
and we realize that that could not be further
from the truth. And so, people finally just

00:08:28.449 --> 00:08:33.080
coming together and demanding action on this,
I think is hugely, hugely important,

00:08:33.080 --> 00:08:35.340
and us actually being able
to do something about it.

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:38.300
[Hank:]
So, you've worked in the side of government a lot,

00:08:38.300 --> 00:08:43.460
and you've seen politics functioning,
you've seen government functioning,

00:08:43.460 --> 00:08:49.040
there are things that are broken about this, but
of course, I think the response to that can't be like,

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:53.540
"Okay, well, let's abandon it then.
Let's continue to hobble it. Let's ignore it."

00:08:53.540 --> 00:08:56.480
It has to be, "Fix it.".
So how do we fix it?

00:08:56.480 --> 00:08:59.260
[Silence]
[Hank laughs]

00:08:59.260 --> 00:09:01.720
[Maya:] Well...
[Hank laughs]

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:05.160
[Maya:]
I think a piece of it is, it's people.

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:12.280
It's people getting involved for the right reasons.
The people right now who are having this be one

00:09:12.290 --> 00:09:16.600
of their formative political experiences,
they are going to be qualitatively different

00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:22.400
types of leaders, because seeing something
like this and having that be the thing that sparks,

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:27.980
"I want to make change." So, I think
that, folks, that if you're called to want

00:09:27.980 --> 00:09:32.260
to do something better
in a moment of crisis,

00:09:32.260 --> 00:09:35.820
go run, do something,
get involved.

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:41.040
Because if that's the thing that sparks your interest
in making things better, you are one of the

00:09:41.050 --> 00:09:47.610
people we want making things better. I think
beyond that, there are things about our politics

00:09:47.610 --> 00:09:52.480
that are broken because...
because there is -

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:55.680
I mean, to put simply,
there's a cost to entry, right?

00:09:55.680 --> 00:09:59.520
I mean, any time
there is a system where

00:09:59.529 --> 00:10:04.990
if you have resources, your problems get greater
attention. And if you don't have those resources,

00:10:04.990 --> 00:10:10.259
your problems get deprioritized. We are not
at a system that is best equipped to deal

00:10:10.259 --> 00:10:14.870
with the issues that impact the most of us,
because most of us do not have those kinds

00:10:14.870 --> 00:10:20.070
of resources. We have to fix what is, I think,
a baseline issue. And that is that our politics

00:10:20.070 --> 00:10:27.660
are way too consumed by, who can get money
into the system and therefore have their issues

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:32.140
looked at first. And there's obviously campaign
finance reform solutions everywhere,

00:10:32.140 --> 00:10:36.320
but I think acknowledging that is a big part of
what we need to do.

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:40.100
I also think we have to
step away from this idea that,

00:10:40.100 --> 00:10:46.560
what I was saying before that when
we talk about issues like racial justice,

00:10:46.560 --> 00:10:51.260
that we are talking about issues that only
are relevant to communities of color,

00:10:51.260 --> 00:10:56.400
and as a result, they can be pushed off to the
side. I think that one of the tough things

00:10:56.410 --> 00:11:00.801
about a moment like this is that we're talking
about policing, but we're also talking about

00:11:00.801 --> 00:11:06.410
a system of white supremacy, and we're talking
about just a very fundamental difference in

00:11:06.410 --> 00:11:11.020
the ways that people interact in the society,
and how they feel protected by the society,

00:11:11.020 --> 00:11:18.070
and is based on race. And we have conditioned
ourselves to think of that as such a divisive topic

00:11:18.070 --> 00:11:21.540
that we don't even want to talk about it
until we're confronted with something like this.

00:11:21.540 --> 00:11:26.700
And then we're almost surprised that we are not
super good at talking about it with each other.

00:11:26.700 --> 00:11:28.420
[Hank and Maya laugh]

00:11:28.420 --> 00:11:33.300
Like we give ourselves no practice,
and then biggest things happen and

00:11:33.300 --> 00:11:36.520
people are awkward around it, or people say
the wrong thing, or people you know -

00:11:36.520 --> 00:11:41.050
And look, there are just some people that -
Obviously, there are some people

00:11:41.050 --> 00:11:46.720
who do not care and are not worried that they
don't know what to say in this moment,

00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:51.600
but I'm not really thinking about them. I'm more
thinking about the folks... genuinely are...

00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:56.940
see a moment like this, feel the helpessness,
white folks who maybe haven't been as conscious

00:11:56.949 --> 00:12:00.060
of some of this stuff and are having their
eyes open, but they don't know where to go,

00:12:00.060 --> 00:12:02.380
and they don't know where to start, and they
don't want to say the wrong thing,

00:12:02.380 --> 00:12:05.640
so they don't say anything.
And it's read differently.

00:12:05.640 --> 00:12:08.540
So I think we've created
this really weird quagmire

00:12:08.540 --> 00:12:12.590
around the way that we approach and
talk about race, that we only talk about it

00:12:12.590 --> 00:12:16.920
in moments of crisis when the temperatures
are already so high. And the only people that

00:12:16.920 --> 00:12:22.160
feel 100% comfortable talking about it are
people that study it and write about it and

00:12:22.160 --> 00:12:27.130
talk about it, and people who don't care how
they sound talking about it. And so basically,

00:12:27.130 --> 00:12:32.120
it is people who are racist or don't care
about sounding racist, talking to people who

00:12:32.120 --> 00:12:35.780
make this their career to talk about. And
that's our national conversation around things.

00:12:35.780 --> 00:12:38.960
And so, I think some of it
is almost just letting ourselves -

00:12:38.960 --> 00:12:42.760
challenging ourselves to have the awkward
conversations. I think it goes back to this

00:12:42.760 --> 00:12:49.360
question of grace. I think if people can come
in good faith and get grace in return,

00:12:49.360 --> 00:12:56.800
I think that some of these conversations can get a
lot more comfortable and a lot more calm.

00:12:56.810 --> 00:13:00.591
I mean, it doesn't mean they're not going
to be awkward, because they are, and they're

00:13:00.591 --> 00:13:03.889
going to be tough, and they're going to be
courageous conversations you have to have

00:13:03.889 --> 00:13:09.339
with people. But I do think that we have to
approach it as it's a necessity, we can't

00:13:09.340 --> 00:13:13.440
wait until it's this
to engage in it at all.

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:16.600
And I'm realizing more and more
how much that still happens.

00:13:16.600 --> 00:13:19.620
[Hank:] Because it is such an
important and big thing in our society,

00:13:19.620 --> 00:13:23.940
I think it is important to
be curious about it, engage with it.

00:13:23.940 --> 00:13:29.160
Because I think that you can't help if you're not
at all informed, and you also can't be like,

00:13:29.160 --> 00:13:33.500
"Okay, the first step of me helping is to
place the burden on someone else to help inform me."

00:13:33.500 --> 00:13:36.100
[Maya:] Right?
[Hank:] But like, you know...

00:13:36.100 --> 00:13:40.199
You can't interface with anything
if you don't know about it. And so I think

00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:44.360
that that learning process is really important.
It should be a bigger part of our education system,

00:13:44.360 --> 00:13:49.220
but it should also be a part of what
we do as citizens.

00:13:49.220 --> 00:13:54.220
It should be just something
that we feel is part of...

00:13:54.220 --> 00:13:58.380
not even necessarily just an obligation,
but something that we should be passionate about.

00:13:58.380 --> 00:14:01.200
[Maya:]
What you just described is, I think,

00:14:01.200 --> 00:14:04.160
one of the tensions
that lies at the core of this.

00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:08.980
[Hank:] Yeah, one of them.
[Maya:] There are people who want to learn and

00:14:08.980 --> 00:14:15.120
their immediate thought is, "Oh, well, I'll ask someone."
Because, we do that with a lot of things in life.

00:14:15.120 --> 00:14:19.240
"So I'll ask someone who seems
to know about this," and then immediately

00:14:19.250 --> 00:14:22.970
that puts a burden on someone else. Sometimes
that is a burden that people are willing to

00:14:22.970 --> 00:14:27.350
take on, and sometimes it's not. There's no
magic ingredient as to when that happens.

00:14:27.350 --> 00:14:31.910
And so, I think a safe way to do it is,
people always say, "Well, Google and read."

00:14:31.910 --> 00:14:36.600
And I think that that's great advice,
and I understand why that's people's advice.

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:40.300
But I also appreciate that
it's not like this stuff is

00:14:40.300 --> 00:14:47.019
super accessible if you genuinely
are like, "I need to start from ground zero."

00:14:47.020 --> 00:14:51.880
I think there are books out there that try
really, really hard to do that. There's a book called,

00:14:51.880 --> 00:14:55.740
So You Want to Talk About Race,
that I think is incredible and does a really good job,

00:14:55.740 --> 00:15:00.260
I think, from a, "Let's meet people
where they are standpoint." But I don't think

00:15:00.279 --> 00:15:05.540
every book or article about racial justice
starts from a standpoint of [inaudible]

00:15:05.540 --> 00:15:09.290
regardless of your level. There's a disconnect
there that I think we have to acknowledge

00:15:09.290 --> 00:15:15.149
that sometimes we are saying, "We want people
to learn." And that means that there are people

00:15:15.149 --> 00:15:18.260
who are curious and don't quite know where
to go with that.

00:15:18.260 --> 00:15:22.940
And so, again,
I think it varies, but I also

00:15:22.949 --> 00:15:32.079
think that a big way to the handle that is
having diverse friend groups. Because if you

00:15:32.079 --> 00:15:37.620
hang with people and already have emotional
ties and a trust level, and they know that

00:15:37.620 --> 00:15:42.680
your intentions are good, and you ask them
questions, people are going to be more willing

00:15:42.680 --> 00:15:45.529
to take on that burden, they're going to be
more willing to give that grace, they're going

00:15:45.529 --> 00:15:49.209
to be more understanding of, you didn't get
the vocabulary quite right, but I knew where

00:15:49.209 --> 00:15:57.389
you were going. But I think that it's a chicken
and the egg thing because it is way too easy

00:15:57.389 --> 00:16:02.620
for [inaudible] to be segregated,
especially for white folks. And then for people

00:16:02.620 --> 00:16:06.680
to look around and say, "Oh wait, I don't
have anyone I'm close to I can ask about this.

00:16:06.680 --> 00:16:09.540
Let me ask this random person."
And that's when -

00:16:09.540 --> 00:16:10.880
[Hank and Maya laugh]

00:16:10.880 --> 00:16:13.060
[Maya:] And that's when there's -

00:16:13.060 --> 00:16:20.200
So, I mean, it's tough, but it feels like the answer really
does seem to be community,  it's closer connection,

00:16:20.200 --> 00:16:23.900
because we can do that stuff when we're already in
community with each other.

00:16:23.900 --> 00:16:26.820
[Hank:]
One thing that we do every year here is

00:16:26.820 --> 00:16:30.000
we have a census that we run
and I usually analyze it.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:32.200
[Maya:]
I've taken that census before, yes.

00:16:32.200 --> 00:16:35.220
[Hank:]
Okay, thank you. And I usually analyze it before now,

00:16:35.220 --> 00:16:39.959
but haven't, because things got busy this year.
We ask a question about race

00:16:39.960 --> 00:16:44.200
in that census, I think, that you were probably
aware, coming into this conversation that

00:16:44.200 --> 00:16:53.240
the majority of the audience would be white folks.
So thinking about that, and introducing

00:16:53.240 --> 00:16:58.939
this topic, maybe to people who have been really
apprehensive about it, or genuinely are coming

00:16:58.939 --> 00:17:04.960
at it from a good place, but feel very anxious,
because it's a completely understandable thing

00:17:04.960 --> 00:17:14.100
to be anxious about. One thing that I see
is two bad reactions to the concept of privilege.

00:17:14.100 --> 00:17:19.760
One is, "I definitely don't have privilege, I have all
these hard things in my life, and so that's fake."

00:17:19.760 --> 00:17:25.460
And the second is, "I have privilege,
I feel guilty about it, and I will

00:17:25.460 --> 00:17:31.210
then move through my life with guilt," but
no positive anything on the other side of that?

00:17:31.210 --> 00:17:34.140
And so it's like,
"Oh, so I've been given this negative emotion,

00:17:34.140 --> 00:17:36.800
and I will just carry it around
and then do all my normal things."

00:17:36.800 --> 00:17:45.080
I am very resistant to the idea that what you have
is something to feel ashamed of,

00:17:45.080 --> 00:17:49.720
because I just think that we're all
in the situation that we're in.

00:17:49.720 --> 00:17:53.940
But I heard Baratunde Thurston say,
this week in a podcast that,

00:17:53.940 --> 00:17:59.560
"It's not like where everybody goes around
and says to Superman, "Stop using your power."

00:17:59.560 --> 00:18:06.740
They say, "Use your power for good stuff."
And so, that really resonates with me

00:18:06.740 --> 00:18:12.240
that this is about what you have, and what you have isn't
about how you should feel about what you have,

00:18:12.240 --> 00:18:15.140
it's about what you should do with what you have.
[Maya:] Right.

00:18:15.140 --> 00:18:17.900
So with that in mind,
what should we do with what we have?

00:18:17.910 --> 00:18:21.740
[Maya:]
I love that framing. And I think you're right.

00:18:21.740 --> 00:18:26.100
I mean, there is a knee jerk when you recognize
you have a privilege.

00:18:26.100 --> 00:18:28.920
[Hank:] Sure.
[Maya:] It's sense of - right, it's like

00:18:28.920 --> 00:18:33.840
you feel bad, you feel like, "Well then, but..." 
And I think it's really important to work through that

00:18:33.840 --> 00:18:38.440
because you're right, I think that the other side
of that is if I feel guilty about something,

00:18:38.440 --> 00:18:40.340
I want to hide it,
I don't want to -

00:18:40.340 --> 00:18:42.640
[Hank:] Or be angry or lash out -
[Maya:] Correct.

00:18:42.640 --> 00:18:45.660
[Hank:] Shame is a terrible emotion
that no one wants to feel. Yeah.

00:18:45.660 --> 00:18:49.020
[Maya:] Absolutely. And so,
working through it and acknowledging it

00:18:49.020 --> 00:18:55.100
and saying it out loud, and I
think that that's important in and of itself.

00:18:55.100 --> 00:19:00.260
Because I think you're right. Just having
that shame, it's so disruptive, but then... right?

00:19:00.260 --> 00:19:06.550
There are so many things that come from that. The
recognition of white privilege in these conversations

00:19:06.550 --> 00:19:10.990
means that there are conversations that would
be easier for you to have than for me to have.

00:19:10.990 --> 00:19:16.410
No matter how many times I talk about race
a lot, I write about race a lot, I tend to

00:19:16.410 --> 00:19:23.240
be a pretty effusive, friendly person. I try really hard
to say, "I'm still me when I'm talking about race."

00:19:23.240 --> 00:19:29.120
But inevitably, as soon as I start talking
about race, there's more defensiveness

00:19:29.120 --> 00:19:35.800
that I feel than if I were just talking about something fun
or something that people just want to talk to me -

00:19:35.800 --> 00:19:39.160
[Hank and Maya laugh]
[Hank:] Yeah!

00:19:39.160 --> 00:19:43.350
[Maya:] But it happens, no matter and there's -
And I feel like I've gotten to a point where

00:19:43.350 --> 00:19:50.330
there's no amount of, "I want to be super accessible
about it that is going to completely relieve that,"

00:19:50.330 --> 00:19:54.540
because as soon as it's coming from me,
there is an assumption of,

00:19:54.540 --> 00:20:00.380
"Okay, I've got to say the right thing, and I don't know
as much, so I can't say anything," and everything else

00:20:00.380 --> 00:20:05.280
that comes along with that. Because of that, I pay a
bigger penalty for bringing that kind of stuff up.

00:20:05.280 --> 00:20:09.780
One of the things that white privilege is
incredibly useful at doing is

00:20:09.780 --> 00:20:14.400
helping to talk about some of the issues
that people of color can't bring up.

00:20:14.400 --> 00:20:19.310
And so, being the one to talk about this,
taking a platform that you have,

00:20:19.310 --> 00:20:25.460
and elevating work that's being done and voices
and stories that wouldn't otherwise get that platform,

00:20:25.460 --> 00:20:30.880
acknowledging that there are ways
that privilege contributed to an ability to

00:20:30.880 --> 00:20:36.180
build a platform, and therefore will bring the
next folks up. That kind of stuff is so valuable,

00:20:36.180 --> 00:20:41.340
and it's so important, and it's only going to
come if people feel comfortable saying,

00:20:41.340 --> 00:20:48.800
"Yes, I have privilege that has helped me. I am going
to make sure that I use that and help other people."

00:20:48.800 --> 00:20:52.560
I mean, I think there's a lot tied up with it,
but this idea that

00:20:52.560 --> 00:20:57.420
if you acknowledge privilege, it means you're somehow
undermining yourself, you haven't worked hard -

00:20:57.420 --> 00:20:59.460
[Hank and Maya laugh]

00:20:59.460 --> 00:21:04.340
[Maya:] But it's like it's either or, that it's like
you did it 1000% by yourself, or

00:21:04.340 --> 00:21:07.240
privilege is the only reason you are here versus here.

00:21:07.240 --> 00:21:09.480
[Hank:] Yeah.
[Maya:] And getting to....

00:21:09.480 --> 00:21:12.320
Please, you look like
you had on thought on that.

00:21:12.320 --> 00:21:14.440
[Hank:]
My thought was that you're saying,

00:21:14.440 --> 00:21:18.500
if you're recognizing your privilege that you're
somehow hurting yourself, you're saying like,

00:21:18.500 --> 00:21:22.720
"Okay, I have privilege, and so I'm going to start
succeeding less," or something.

00:21:22.720 --> 00:21:24.180
[Maya:] Right.

00:21:24.180 --> 00:21:29.300
[Hank:] And I see people who think that.
But to me, it's like

00:21:29.300 --> 00:21:33.060
helping people is the best part
of being successful.

00:21:33.060 --> 00:21:38.700
It's like, being able to meet people who are amazing
and be like, people should pay attention to

00:21:38.700 --> 00:21:43.010
this great person. And it's also well known,
psychologically, that helping other people

00:21:43.010 --> 00:21:48.010
makes you happier than helping yourself. And
so, it's like, "But why wouldn't we do it?"

00:21:48.010 --> 00:21:51.950
Of course, our brains don't think that, but
that's how we react.

00:21:51.950 --> 00:21:57.360
There's this fairly famous study that
if you give somebody 50 bucks

00:21:57.360 --> 00:22:01.880
and you say, "Spend it on yourself,"
or you say, "Spend it on someone else,"

00:22:01.880 --> 00:22:05.840
that at the end of the day,
they would all prefer to spend it on themselves.

00:22:05.840 --> 00:22:08.540
But at the end of the day, they're happier
when they spend it on someone else.

00:22:08.540 --> 00:22:11.140
[Maya:] Right.
[Hank:] And so like,

00:22:11.140 --> 00:22:17.740
being mindful and aware of that, and also
having had that experience a number of times,

00:22:17.740 --> 00:22:24.760
I know it is true, even if it doesn't feel
intuitive all the time. So, to me, it's like,

00:22:24.760 --> 00:22:28.920
"Oh, this is so wonderful that I have an opportunity
to use a power that I have."

00:22:28.920 --> 00:22:30.860
[Maya:] Right.
[Hank:] Because that's really what privilege is,

00:22:30.860 --> 00:22:34.660
is it's power.
Should be thinking more about how to use that,

00:22:34.660 --> 00:22:39.260
because it makes me happier
and it makes the world better.

00:22:39.260 --> 00:22:42.160
And those are
the two things I want.

00:22:42.160 --> 00:22:45.620
[Hank and Maya laugh]

00:22:45.620 --> 00:22:49.140
[Maya:]
[inaudible] It's just that easy and clear. I mean, come on.

00:22:49.140 --> 00:22:51.720
[inaudible]
[Hank and Maya laugh]

00:22:51.720 --> 00:22:56.840
[Maya:] It's true. And I also think
it's important to acknowledge that people

00:22:56.840 --> 00:23:00.100
who don't have privilege, in some ways, have
privilege in other places, right?

00:23:00.100 --> 00:23:02.980
[Hank:] Yeah.
[Maya:] I have an awful lot of privilege as a straight,

00:23:02.980 --> 00:23:11.960
cis person, right? And we have the sense of you're either
privileged or you're not, it's binary, right?

00:23:11.961 --> 00:23:16.910
And so, in different settings, I'm the one who
can say, "Wait a minute, there's something

00:23:16.910 --> 00:23:23.360
I can bring up here that my queer friends couldn't,"
or "I can be the one to call out ableism,

00:23:23.360 --> 00:23:28.860
because as an able bodied person, it's easier for me."
Right? So, I think that acknowledging

00:23:28.860 --> 00:23:36.940
that around race conversations, yes, I'm going
to rely a lot more on my white friends who

00:23:36.940 --> 00:23:41.590
want to act in allyship and bring this stuff
up when I can't. But there are going to be

00:23:41.590 --> 00:23:44.081
rooms where I absolutely have
that ability to do it.

00:23:44.081 --> 00:23:47.300
And so, I also think
that helps us sometimes,

00:23:47.300 --> 00:23:51.600
because, again, if we conceptualize it as
you have it, or you don't, that makes it way

00:23:51.600 --> 00:23:57.420
too easy to buy into this model of somehow
it makes me less than, or...

00:23:57.420 --> 00:24:02.220
That it really is situational, and I think it's important that
we talk about it that way.

00:24:02.220 --> 00:24:05.820
[Hank:] My last question here for you is,
what's making you hopeful right now?

00:24:05.820 --> 00:24:09.300
[Maya:]
I will say again, watching the young people

00:24:09.300 --> 00:24:16.060
who are leading this fight is incredibly,
incredibly inspiring.

00:24:16.060 --> 00:24:22.880
It makes me feel like there are people who are seeing
this and are having that spark awakened in them.

00:24:22.880 --> 00:24:30.120
And so, this becomes the issue that our movement is
taking up, that leaders are taking up.

00:24:30.120 --> 00:24:34.860
I think that that is going to be huge. I am seeing
more of these conversations.

00:24:34.860 --> 00:24:42.480
I've gotten a lot of people who've reached out to me
in these moments, who, these are friends that

00:24:42.480 --> 00:24:47.940
I have strong relationships with, who have texted and
done the, "I want to check in. How are you feeling?"

00:24:47.940 --> 00:24:52.460
But have gone further than that have said,
"I'm raising kids and I don't know

00:24:52.460 --> 00:24:57.320
how I should be talking about this, but I know
that I haven't talked to my white kids about this.

00:24:57.320 --> 00:25:02.700
And I know that peers who have Black kids
are already having to have these conversations.

00:25:02.700 --> 00:25:06.260
So, "What do you think, what should I
be doing? What should I be saying?"

00:25:06.260 --> 00:25:12.080
And I'm so grateful that it's like,
"Okay, people want to do this."

00:25:12.080 --> 00:25:17.520
I think that there's so much power in
just being willing to say,

00:25:17.520 --> 00:25:23.040
"Huh, I haven't thought about that before." Again, there's
vulnerability there, because there's the obvious part of,

00:25:23.040 --> 00:25:26.880
"Well, how lucky am I that I never had to think about it."
And you could get stuck in that.

00:25:26.880 --> 00:25:29.910
But instead, people are actually reaching out
and trying to do something different.

00:25:29.910 --> 00:25:34.880
I'm seeing a lot more of the Facebook posts
where people... And my absolute, absolute

00:25:34.880 --> 00:25:39.880
favorite thing is for people when they say,
"Hey, listen, if you're having this moment

00:25:39.880 --> 00:25:43.700
right now, and you're hearing people say Black
lives matter, and you think to yourself,

00:25:43.700 --> 00:25:49.280
'Well, all lives matter,' DM me."
One of the, I think hardest things -

00:25:49.280 --> 00:25:52.220
[Hank:]
Wow, wow. Yeah, yeah, that's great.

00:25:52.220 --> 00:25:55.790
[Maya:]
And it's so critical, because here's the thing.

00:25:55.790 --> 00:25:59.660
You get on social media, and you're like,
"If you're thinking this, unfollow me,

00:25:59.660 --> 00:26:04.660
if you're thinking this, I'm going to say..."
You're going to get a lot more likes and engagement.

00:26:04.660 --> 00:26:11.750
And there's something really satisfying about
just unfollowing the family member who always

00:26:11.750 --> 00:26:19.620
says the wrong thing or who... You get satisfaction
from that, and you get external validation from it.

00:26:19.620 --> 00:26:24.400
But the actual helpful conversation
is one that you can only have in DMs.

00:26:24.400 --> 00:26:29.000
Because it's the conversation where you say, "You
know what? Actually, I used to think this,"

00:26:29.000 --> 00:26:33.560
or "I've actually had moments like this,"
and you make yourself vulnerable so this person

00:26:33.560 --> 00:26:37.760
makes themselves vulnerable back. And you
can actually get to somebody in a way you

00:26:37.760 --> 00:26:40.000
couldn't if [inaudible]
[Hank:] Yeah, yeah. Of course.

00:26:40.000 --> 00:26:43.100
[Maya:] Nobody's changing their mind based on
an argument on someone's timeline on Facebook -

00:26:43.100 --> 00:26:45.280
[Hank:]
Right. Publicly, performatively,

00:26:45.280 --> 00:26:48.580
so that everybody's looking at it
and you can't show any weakness. Yeah.

00:26:48.580 --> 00:26:51.840
[Maya:]
Exactly. It undermines every single thing

00:26:51.840 --> 00:26:55.260
that would be valuable about this conversation.
You can't admit,

00:26:55.260 --> 00:27:00.060
"Huh, I hadn't considered that." You can't do that.
But you can over DM, you can over phone calls.

00:27:00.060 --> 00:27:06.320
And so that I think is so much of what we say when
there's a sense of like, "Go get your people."

00:27:06.320 --> 00:27:13.080
The goal there is not, "Go excoriate on social media
your people." The idea is you...

00:27:13.080 --> 00:27:16.480
[Hank and Maya laugh]

00:27:16.480 --> 00:27:23.200
[Maya:] And I think - look, again, I get it, even from a
standpoint that has nothing to do with

00:27:23.200 --> 00:27:29.000
external validation, it feels good to have somebody say
something awful and you to slam the door in their face.

00:27:29.000 --> 00:27:32.140
There's something satisfying about that,
but it's not helpful.

00:27:32.140 --> 00:27:35.940
And I think that's one of the tough things
to remember about being an ally,

00:27:35.940 --> 00:27:40.820
is that on some level it sucks.
It's not supposed to be easy,

00:27:40.820 --> 00:27:44.960
it's doing a lot of the hard work. It feels good
because you know, you're doing the right thing,

00:27:44.960 --> 00:27:49.980
not because in a moment it's satisfying.
You're actually supposed to do the stuff

00:27:49.980 --> 00:27:54.520
that makes you a little uncomfortable,
and that gives an inch and lets that person

00:27:54.520 --> 00:27:59.300
come in enough, and then say,
"Look, I get it. And here's how I got here."

00:27:59.300 --> 00:28:05.380
Because that's the conversation that stays with them.
And so I've been really, really excited every time

00:28:05.380 --> 00:28:09.440
I see that kind of stuff. Because I know that
the real work has to happen behind the closed doors,

00:28:09.440 --> 00:28:12.700
and it doesn't get the likes, and it
doesn't get the retweets,

00:28:12.700 --> 00:28:17.600
but it actually might be the thing that stops someone
from saying it the next time,

00:28:17.600 --> 00:28:22.000
or that has someone think twice
the next time they're in a situation.

00:28:22.000 --> 00:28:24.350
[Hank:]
Well, this has been a really wonderful conversation.

00:28:24.350 --> 00:28:28.560
I appreciate you doing it. Also, thanks for
chatting with me offline a bit this morning.

00:28:28.560 --> 00:28:33.760
So it was great to talk with you.
What was your first Vlogbrothers video?

00:28:33.760 --> 00:28:36.120
[Maya:]
I saw the Harry Potter song.

00:28:36.120 --> 00:28:38.160
[Hank:] Deathly Hallows? Wow!
[Maya:] Deathly Hallows.

00:28:38.160 --> 00:28:39.900
[Hank:] Wow!
[Maya:] Yes!

00:28:39.900 --> 00:28:42.780
So you've just been a nerd the whole time. I see what's -
[Maya:] I really have been!

00:28:42.780 --> 00:28:46.890
So it was so funny, too,
because I remember I saw it because it was

00:28:46.890 --> 00:28:53.300
featured on YouTube's homepage. I was not
a YouTuber, I did not know about this whole community.

00:28:53.300 --> 00:28:55.640
I saw this, I was like -
[Hank:] Well, it was 2007.

00:28:55.640 --> 00:28:57.160
No one did.
[Maya:] Thank you.

00:28:57.160 --> 00:28:59.980
Thank you for that.
Because we're going to now pretend like

00:28:59.980 --> 00:29:02.220
I definitely know so much
about YouTube.

00:29:02.220 --> 00:29:03.460
[Hank and Maya laugh]

00:29:03.460 --> 00:29:04.760
[Hank:]
Now you're really in.

00:29:04.760 --> 00:29:08.200
[Maya:] I know I'm deep in it.
[laughs]

00:29:08.200 --> 00:29:11.070
I remember, and it was there
and I sent it to my sister.

00:29:11.070 --> 00:29:13.940
And then I just saw that you guys
were doing this thing.

00:29:13.950 --> 00:29:18.050
And I have a sister I'm very close to. And
so, I'm always really excited when siblings

00:29:18.050 --> 00:29:23.790
are doing stuff together. I remember just
going back and I was so, and have remained...

00:29:23.790 --> 00:29:28.790
And this is very genuinely true, I have remained
so impressed by this community that has been built,

00:29:28.790 --> 00:29:32.760
because so much of, I think, what we were just
talking about is people seeking out community

00:29:32.760 --> 00:29:37.000
and trying to have some grace and trying to
have some humility, is not always...

00:29:37.000 --> 00:29:40.160
That doesn't always have a
very natural home online.

00:29:40.160 --> 00:29:45.200
And I really thought here, 
I don't myself make videos or anything,

00:29:45.200 --> 00:29:49.020
but I remember there was a period of time where
people would make their own videos and share them.

00:29:49.020 --> 00:29:53.400
And I felt this very -
Y'all had cultivated and brought together

00:29:53.400 --> 00:29:58.240
a group of people who genuinely seem
to want to just do good things together. And

00:29:58.240 --> 00:30:02.680
I've loved that. I loved the idea of the projects,
and there's a charity now!

00:30:02.680 --> 00:30:06.000
Because P - you know what I mean?
[Hank and Maya laugh]

00:30:06.000 --> 00:30:10.800
I've just been so consistently warmed by that,
and have always just been a big fan.

00:30:10.800 --> 00:30:15.180
So I'm so grateful that you reached out, and
I'm so happy to have had this conversation.

00:30:15.180 --> 00:30:17.810
[Hank:]
Oh, thank you so much! And I'm so grateful

00:30:17.810 --> 00:30:21.850
that you're doing all of the work you're doing.
I'm grateful you're a part of this community,

00:30:21.850 --> 00:30:26.500
but also other communities, and all this stuff
you're doing to help make the world

00:30:26.500 --> 00:30:29.780
a bit of a better place.
Where can people find you?

00:30:29.780 --> 00:30:33.060
[Maya:] I'm on Twitter.
[Hank:] Oh no!

00:30:33.060 --> 00:30:35.400
[Hank and Maya laugh]

00:30:35.400 --> 00:30:38.670
[Maya:] I'm @MayaRupert on Twitter.
I think I'm the same on Facebook.

00:30:38.670 --> 00:30:40.900
[Hank:]
So you're not on TikTok yet?

00:30:40.900 --> 00:30:45.310
[Maya:]
I had interns teach me TikTok. I did one.

00:30:45.310 --> 00:30:50.800
I wish we had saved the outtakes. Because
it was me just consistently not understanding

00:30:50.800 --> 00:30:53.960
the vehicle at all.
[laughs]

00:30:53.960 --> 00:30:56.700
[Hank:] Oh my God.
[Maya:] Yeah, this is - I feel like -

00:30:56.700 --> 00:31:00.880
My social media is limited,
but I often am on Twitter.

00:31:00.880 --> 00:31:02.340
[Hank:]:
Well, thank you so much, Maya.

00:31:02.340 --> 00:31:03.920
[Maya:]
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

00:31:03.920 --> 00:31:07.760
[Hank and Maya laugh]
[Maya:] That was awesome.

00:31:07.760 --> 00:31:09.840
[Hank:] If you want to check out
So You Want to Talk About Race,

00:31:09.840 --> 00:31:12.080
the book she was talking about,
that's linked to the description.

00:31:12.080 --> 00:31:16.400
Also, 8cantwait.org.
The number 8can'twait.org.

00:31:16.400 --> 00:31:20.220
It's a fantastic resource, makes it easy
to understand how to start

00:31:20.220 --> 00:31:24.380
or continue work in advocacy,
trying to get some things changed.

